@@ -0,0 +1,65 @@
+# Optional SM70 gated RMSNorm extension
+
+The PP decode changes do not require this optional extension. Production keeps
+`sm70_rmsnorm_gated_exact=false`: the existing Triton kernel was faster for larger
+batches in the measured PP7 workload. The extension allows explicit comparison
+of the original FP32 reduction schedule at up to 3,072 rows of 128 FP16 values.
+
+The additional library registers `vllm_pp7::rmsnorm_gated_exact_out` and is loaded
+from `vllm/_pp7_fusion.so`. It never replaces the main vLLM native extension.
+Rows 1–192 still use the existing `_C::sm70_rmsnorm_gated_exact_out` operator.
+The CUDA dispatch falls back to Triton if the additional library is missing.
+
+## Build in a separate checkout
+
+Use the checkout's existing CUDA-enabled PyTorch environment, a compatible CUDA
+toolkit with `nvcc`, Ninja, and a C++17 compiler. Set `CUDA_HOME` if the toolkit is
+not discoverable. The validated deployment used PyTorch 2.10.0+cu128, its C++11
+ABI, GCC 11.4, and nvcc 12.9.86. Compiler paths and versions are not fixed by the
+script; rebuilding with another toolchain need not produce an identical binary.
+
+```bash
+# Show source, output, architecture, and build settings without importing torch.
+.venv/bin/python tools/build_pp7_fusion.py
+
+# Build only the additional SM70 library; CUDA devices remain hidden.
+.venv/bin/python tools/build_pp7_fusion.py --execute
+
+# Optional overrides:
+.venv/bin/python tools/build_pp7_fusion.py --execute \
+  --cuda-home /path/to/cuda --build-dir /path/to/build
+```
+
+The kernel is compiled for SM70 with `-O3 --fmad=false` and without fast-math.
+Do not add contraction or fast-math flags: the arithmetic order is intentional.
+The builder refuses symlink outputs and existing binaries with different bytes.
+Use a separate checkout when an installed binary must remain available. The
+normal repository build does not build this optional library automatically.
+
+## Validation and opt-in
+
+CPU routing and fallback checks do not require a GPU. The focused conftest
+boundary avoids loading the repository-wide optional test dependencies:
+
+```bash
+CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' .venv/bin/python -m pytest \
+  tests/kernels/test_pp7_fusion_candidate.py \
+  tests/kernels/test_actual_norm_dispatch.py \
+  --confcutdir=tests/kernels -k cpu -q
+```
+
+Run GPU checks only on an idle SM70 device, after building the extension:
+
+```bash
+CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 PP7_FUSION_GPU_TESTS=1 .venv/bin/python -m pytest \
+  tests/kernels/test_pp7_fusion_candidate.py \
+  tests/kernels/test_actual_norm_dispatch.py \
+  --confcutdir=tests/kernels -k gpu -q
+```
+
+For experiments, set `sm70_rmsnorm_gated_exact=true` in `--kernel-config`, keeping
+the other required configuration keys. Admission requires SM70, contiguous FP16
+inputs with 128 columns, no bias or grouping, normalization before gating, and
+SiLU/swish or sigmoid. Keep the flag false for the validated production setting;
+microbenchmark gains against a multi-operation reference do not establish an
+improvement over the existing fused Triton implementation.
diff --git a/docs/deployment/pp_transport_diagnostics.md b/docs/deployment/pp_transport_diagnostics.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..7dbc181c90ae64c826a150d993225370541c17fa
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment/pp_transport_diagnostics.md
@@ -0,0 +1,45 @@
+# PP transport diagnostics
+
+Set `pp_transport_metrics_interval` to a positive integer in
+`--additional-config` to log cumulative counters per pipeline rank every N
+nonempty forward batches. Zero (the default) disables hidden-state metrics.
+For example, preserve other deployment options and add:
+
+```json
+{"pp_transport_metrics_interval": 256}
+```
+
+`PP_TRANSPORT_METRICS` reports decode-static hits, the first scheduler condition
+causing a fallback, tensor-message counts, and payload bytes by direction and
+protocol. Static byte counts exclude local graph-padding rows. Dynamic counts
+include the tensors actually submitted; neither count includes serialized
+metadata, NCCL framing, or physical network amplification. `legacy_static`
+identifies the older fixed-shape transfer independently of the decode option.
+
+`PP_FEEDBACK_METRICS` reports submitted feedback batches, collectives, payload
+bytes, packing mode, and queue depth. The packed path submits one collective per
+feedback batch; the original path submits two. Submitted work is not a count of
+request outputs accepted by the scheduler. A canceled generation can discard
+received feedback after its transport has already occurred.
+
+`Worker.get_pp_transport_metrics()` returns the latest counters through the
+existing engine collective-RPC interface. It adds no public HTTP route. The
+ordinary service does not need developer mode to emit periodic logs. Snapshot
+reads and counting inspect tensor metadata only; they do not synchronize CUDA.
+The host send-wait counter measures CPU time spent in `Work.wait()`, which may
+enqueue stream dependencies; it is **not** CUDA transfer latency or proof of a
+communication bottleneck. GPU overlap analysis requires a separate trace.
+
+For a transport comparison, test all four combinations of
+`pp_decode_static_transfer` and `pp_packed_feedback`: 00, 10, 01, and 11. Keep
+model, GPU order, layer partition, MTP settings, queue depth, metrics interval,
+and token-ID inputs identical. Prime every fresh process before measuring;
+report cold prefill separately. Include multiple warm repeats, prefix hits,
+MTP acceptance, preemptions, temperatures/clocks, TTFT, end-to-end wall time, and
+the common interval during which all concurrent streams are producing tokens.
+Do not compare a selected steady interval with the entire common interval.
+
+Before timing, run the [real NCCL lifecycle regressions](../../tests/v1/worker/pp_nccl_lifecycle.md).
+The forced-reset tests additionally verify that worker request generations are
+retired before delayed feedback is consumed, including requests that only
+resume in a later batch. Ordinary preemption retains its prior output ordering.
