--- a/docs/design/mixed_prefill_latency.md
+++ b/docs/design/mixed_prefill_latency.md
@@ -5,7 +5,7 @@ for a large prefill chunk. Its decode/verification rows may use fast kernels
 and still wait seconds for the rest of the step to finish.
 
 The scheduler now reserves tokens for eligible resident decoders and limits
-the aggregate prefill work sharing their step. The default GPU step latency
+the aggregate prefill work sharing their step. The default step latency
 target is 250 ms. A conservative initial budget is adjusted using completed
 GPU measurements, so the policy follows the actual model, device, parallelism,
 and context instead of an exact model-shape contract. This is a soft target,
@@ -15,8 +15,40 @@ budget is retained. Pure decode retains its existing FULL CUDA graph route.
 `--mixed-prefill-step-latency-ms` changes the target; zero disables the policy
 for comparison. There is no extra environment variable. GPU timing creates
 events only for controlled mixed steps, checks their readiness without waiting,
-and returns plain completed timing data to the scheduler. Pipeline parallel
-and indivisible multimodal chunks retain their existing scheduling behavior.
+and returns plain completed timing data to the scheduler for PP=1.
+Indivisible multimodal chunks retain their existing scheduling behavior.
+
+## Pipeline parallel feedback
+
+For PP>1 the engine measures submission-to-result-consumption latency on its
+monotonic host clock. Each `SchedulerOutput` owns its start timestamp, including
+when structured-output sampling is deferred. This is an observed end-to-end
+latency including queued work, communication, sampling and engine consumption
+delay, not GPU compute time. No new device synchronization or result wait is
+introduced. Workers skip local CUDA timing for these batches: the last stage's
+events are not a complete pipeline measurement.
+
+PP also limits prefill queued behind resident decoders that are temporarily
+ineligible because their feedback is still in flight. These controlled batches
+can have zero local decode rows. Execution eligibility, MTP placeholders and
+recurrent-state feedback ordering are unchanged. Cancellation removes the
+resident from protection through the normal scheduler request lifecycle.
+
+PP starts with 32 aggregate prefill tokens per controlled batch, subject to the
+configured minimum and maximum. This avoids an initially large chunk before
+pipeline measurements are available. A stalled PP batch can reduce the budget
+even when another scheduling limit makes the chunk smaller than the budget;
+short fast boundary chunks still cannot increase it. Small budgets can grow by
+at least one 16-token quantum when measured cost permits, so a temporary stall
+cannot permanently trap the controller at its minimum.
+
+The target remains soft: queued batches may already have been submitted, and
+long-context decode alone can exceed it. Smaller prefill chunks favor resident
+decode continuity at the cost of incoming prompt TTFT. Pure prefill without
+resident decoders retains its normal budget, and pure decode retains the same
+CUDA graph route. CPU regressions cover PP7/MTP cadence, queued and deferred
+feedback, cancellation and budget adaptation. The GPU performance measurements
+below predate PP support and do not establish its throughput or latency gains.
 
 ## Small chunks and recurrent state
 
@@ -111,3 +143,26 @@ and computation overlap is not implemented in this change. Broad scheduler
 tests that import Llava were unavailable in the test environment because of
 an unrelated Transformers Pixtral import mismatch; focused cache, state,
 scheduling, kernel and packaging tests provide the change's validation.
+
+## Context-only draft work and PP observations
+
+An incomplete prompt chunk still populates the Eagle/MTP draft model's KV and
+recurrent state. With a single data-parallel replica, it now stops after that
+prefill pass instead of performing the remaining speculative decode steps:
+these proposals cannot be consumed until the prompt finishes. The feedback
+keeps its normal fixed-width shape and contains zero-filled unused proposals.
+Final prompt chunks, mixed batches, DP collectives, profiling and warmup keep
+their existing draft path. No device-to-host check or synchronization is added.
+
+PP timing also observes standalone prefill batches. A zero control budget marks
+these observations so they cannot train the mixed-prefill latency controller.
+The engine emits one batch-latency sample per ten seconds, including token
+counts and the mixed budget, without logging prompts or request identifiers.
+The measured latency includes queued work and PP communication; it is not a
+per-stage GPU kernel measurement. Pure decode does not create timing events.
+
+Larger standalone prefill capacity can be configured through
+`max_num_batched_tokens` while the separate mixed-prefill budget continues to
+reserve resident decode capacity. The capacity must still be checked against
+the deployment's peak memory. CPU tests cover these execution and scheduling
+contracts; they do not establish a GPU throughput improvement.
diff --git a/docs/design/native_gguf_adapters.md b/docs/design/native_gguf_adapters.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..3143428fea692ffa4bd05c26731ba0e367cac17b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/design/native_gguf_adapters.md
@@ -0,0 +1,84 @@
+# Native GGUF architecture adapters
+
+Native GGUF loading reconstructs configuration from checkpoint metadata and
+keeps architecture-specific tensor conversions separate from the shared loader.
+The loader handles shard discovery, pipeline-stage filtering, quantization policy,
+and weight-loading orchestration. Adapters describe the checkpoint contract.
+
+## Adding an architecture
+
+1. Add a configuration builder under `vllm/transformers_utils/gguf_configs/` and
+   register it in `_CONFIG_BUILDERS` in `gguf_config.py`. Register its configuration
+   class in `_CONFIG_CLASSES` when Transformers does not supply that class.
+   `supports_native_gguf_config()` is the shared admission check for both legacy
+   builders and this registry. Missing dimensions and unsupported layouts must
+   fail with a specific error; avoid defaults that silently change the model.
+2. Add a text adapter in `vllm/model_executor/model_loader/gguf_adapters/` and
+   register its lazy class reference in `_TEXT_ADAPTERS`. Its `build_name_map`
+   checks the complete tensor directory before payloads are read. Record unused
+   prediction layers explicitly. Define exact inverse conversions using the
+   exporter as the reference, including fused projections, tensor order, and
+   parameters that require FP32 storage or computation.
+3. Reuse the shared tensor reader and dense shape/dtype checks in `common.py`.
+   Preserve original expert quantization blocks when supported. Apply pipeline
+   filtering before touching payloads; a transformation combining several source
+   tensors must reject an incomplete filtered group.
+4. For multimodal models, register a `VisionAdapterSpec` in `vision.py`. The spec
+   declares the wrapper's language prefix and lazily imports the vision adapter.
+   A vision adapter exposes `name_map`, `unquantized_modules`, and
+   `weights(dtype, filtered_map=None)`. Construct the vision tower only on the
+   pipeline stage that consumes images, and verify that every expected vision
+   parameter was loaded. Processor assets and their geometry remain a separate
+   requirement from the text checkpoint.
+5. Reuse a native tokenizer preprocessing profile when its metadata agrees.
+   Otherwise add a profile to `vllm/tokenizers/gguf.py` and compare real token IDs
+   with the checkpoint's tokenizer. Segmentation, Unicode normalization, special
+   tokens, and BOS/EOS insertion are independent parts of that contract.
+
+## Completeness and numerical checks
+
+An adapter may implement `validate_loaded_parameters(model, loaded)` to check
+actual runtime parameter coverage after `model.load_weights()` and before weight
+postprocessing. A complete name map alone cannot prove that a model's loader
+accepted every tensor. Fused expert storage needs a per-projection loading ledger
+because its public placeholder parameter names do not enumerate expert payloads.
+
+GLM5-Next uses this hook for dense parameters, native expert banks, and the FP32
+indexer gate copy. Its native and canonical GGUF MoE paths apply the model's
+SwiGLU clamp. Fused activation kernels without a clamp argument are bypassed;
+TurboMind expert-cache prefill rejects this configuration and directs users to
+MMQ cache prefill instead. Existing models without a clamp retain their dispatch.
+
+Keep the checkpoint's declared activation precision separate from its GGUF
+storage type and the resolved runtime dtype. A BF16-trained draft can exceed
+FP16 range even when its weights are stored as F16 and its GPU supports BF16.
+DFlash2 uses its range-preserving path when that checkpoint executes in FP16,
+including a draft that inherits FP16 from a heterogeneous target pipeline.
+Native BF16 execution retains its normal path. Check intermediate activations
+and actual draft acceptance; correct target answers alone can hide invalid
+proposals that the verifier rejects on every step.
+
+Speculative verification also changes the history a recurrent cache must retain.
+The KPool tail keeps `round_up(pool_size + speculative_tokens, pool_size)`
+positions so rejected proposals cannot overwrite the preceding committed pool.
+Its seed and update kernels use physical page strides, including allocator
+padding. Validate repeated reject/accept cycles and slot reuse against sequential
+decoding before increasing context length.
+
+## Validation for a new model
+
+- Check a real tensor inventory, including shapes, data types, optional tensors,
+  and separate prediction layers. Add failure cases for missing and unknown names.
+- Test conversions with small real GGUF files whose values distinguish transposes
+  and slice order. Assert that skipped pipeline stages never read their payloads.
+- Compare native tokenizer output against a pinned tokenizer revision.
+- Exercise the model's real parameter loaders and reject incomplete coverage.
+- Test attention, quantization, and graph replay on every admitted GPU architecture
+  before expanding capability selection. Reusing a Python/Triton path does not
+  make an architecture-specific native CUDA binary portable.
+- Finish with full-model generation, multimodal requests where supported, and
+  speculative-decoding quality checks before enabling a persistent deployment.
+
+Hardware capability helpers should expose a literal capability set that the
+attention-backend documentation generator can resolve. This keeps generated
+support tables aligned with runtime admission without duplicating model rules.
